=== layout: section

# Gene Order and Map Distances


=== layout: one-panel

# What Is the Gene Order?

@body

![Observed recombination: CD 30%, CH 20%, DH 40%, shown on the same percentage scale.](assets/lect08f-three_point_testcross_bpw/pairwise_results.svg)

Which pair has the largest recombination frequency?


=== layout: one-panel

# Three Choices, One Middle Gene

@body

| Middle Gene | Gene Order | Same Map Reversed |
| --- | --- | --- |
| C | D - *C* - H | H - *C* - D |
| D | C - *D* - H | H - *D* - C |
| H | C - *H* - D | D - *H* - C |

Reversing a map gives the same order: only *three choices*.

D-H has the largest recombination frequency. If D and H are the ends,
*which gene is in the middle?*


=== layout: one-panel

# Place the Middle Gene

@body

![D-C-H is the gene order; H-C-D is equivalent.](assets/lect08f-three_point_testcross_bpw/gene_order_middle.svg)


=== layout: one-panel

# Why Don't the Results Add?

@body

![A proportional map: D to C is 30 cM, C to H is 20 cM; the sum is 50 cM while D-H observed recombination is 40%.](assets/lect08f-three_point_testcross_bpw/gene_map.svg)

Which offspring count for both adjacent intervals, but not for D-H?


=== layout: subsection

# Account for Double Crossovers


=== layout: one-panel

# Two Exchanges Can Hide Recombination

@body

![Trace a double-recombinant chromatid across two exchanges, one in CD and one in CH. It inherits + at D and H from the same parental chromatid, with + at C from the other.](assets/lect08f-three_point_testcross_bpw/two_exchanges.svg)

@citation

Two participating nonsister chromatids are shown schematically in D-C-H order.


=== layout: one-panel

# The Outer Genes Still Look Parental

@body

![Parental and double-recombinant combinations in D-C-H map order: +c+ versus +++, and d+h versus dch. Only the middle C allele differs.](assets/lect08f-three_point_testcross_bpw/double_recombinants.svg)

With C covered, each double recombinant matches a parental D-H pair.

@citation

Alleles are now displayed in chromosome order: D-C-H.


=== layout: one-panel

# Count Both Adjacent Intervals

@body

{column-widths="2.5,1,1,1,1"}
| Original Label | Count | [CD]{color=#182033 background-color=#FF8F8F text-align=center} | [CH]{color=#182033 background-color=#8FABFF text-align=center} | [DH]{color=#182033 background-color=#DC9AF4 text-align=center} |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| cdh | 29 | [R]{background-color=#FF8F8F text-align=center} | [R]{background-color=#8FABFF text-align=center} | [P]{background-color=#D6FFAD text-align=center} |
| +++ | 21 | [R]{background-color=#FF8F8F text-align=center} | [R]{background-color=#8FABFF text-align=center} | [P]{background-color=#D6FFAD text-align=center} |

50 / 1,000 &times; 100 = *5%* double recombinants.

These 50 offspring are *already counted in both intervals*: 100 interval counts.

[30]{color=#D40000} + [20]{color=#003FFF} = *50 cM*

[40]{color=#A719DB} + 2 &times; 5 = *50 cM*

@citation

R = recombinant; P = parental for the indicated pair. Original labels use C-D-H order.


